## Why This Is Asked

Introduces bias and fairness in a practical recommender task with limited labels and a new user base.

## Key Concepts

- Fairness metrics: demographic parity, equalized odds
- Calibration under limited labels
- Simple baselines: logistic regression, small decision trees
- Data splits and leakage avoidance
- Lightweight retraining and drift monitoring

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple group CTR calculator
function groupCTR(data, group) {
  const g = data.filter(d => d.group === group);
  const clicks = g.filter(d => d.clicked).length;
  return g.length ? clicks / g.length : 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend with per-group debiasing?
- How would you validate fairness on unseen regions?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests system design for real-time fraud scoring at scale with privacy constraints, multi-tenant data sharing, and safe rollbacks.

## Key Concepts
- Streaming feature store and time-window features
- Hybrid inference paths: server-side and on-device
- Federated learning with differential privacy
- Drift detection and canary-based rollout strategies
- Model calibration and per-transaction explainability

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo scoring sketch
function score(tx, globalModel, localModel){
  const feats = extract(tx)
  const s1 = globalModel.predict(feats)
  const s2 = localModel.predict(feats)
  return calibrate((0.6*s1 + 0.4*s2));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle label latency for online updates?
- What metrics trigger rollback and how would you test rollbacks?

## Why This Question Is Asked
Evaluates the ability to design real-world, latency-constrained explainability systems that balance privacy and governance requirements in high-stakes ML environments.

## Key Concepts
- Per-instance explanations under strict latency constraints
- Privacy-preserving attribution without raw feature exposure
- Caching, bucketing, and surrogate models for performance optimization
- Drift monitoring and governance compliance

## Code Example
```javascript
function attribution(event) {
  const bucket = features.map(f => bucketize(f.value, f.schema));
  const fingerprint = hash(bucket);
  const cached = cache.get(fingerprint);
  if (cached) return cached;
  const attribution = surrogate.predict(bucket);
  cache.set(fingerprint, attribution);
  return attribution;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses practical NLP pipeline setup, handling class imbalance, and real-world deployment concerns for a beginner-friendly problem.

## Key Concepts
- TF-IDF vectorization with unigrams/bigrams
- Handling imbalanced data via class_weight
- Time-based train/test splits to mimic streaming data
- Evaluation: macro F1 and PR-AUC for imbalanced tasks
- Canary rollout and monitoring basics for production safety

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: outline of pipeline components
function buildPipeline(){
  const vectorizer = new TFIDF({ngramRange:[1,2], maxFeatures:100000});
  const model = new LogisticRegression({classWeight:'balanced'});
  return {vectorizer, model};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to multi-class categorization of spam types?
- How would you monitor drift and adjust thresholds in production?

